{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>This is what YHWH spoke, saying: Through them that draw near to Me I am sanctified.</b> And where did He speak? Rather, He spoke in two places. The first, at the assembly at Mount Sinai, where He warned the priests not to gaze more than is fitting — this is what is written (Exodus 19:22): ״וגם הכהנים הנגשים אל ה׳ יתקדשו פן יפרץ בהם ה׳״ — just as we find in Nadab and Abihu, that He broke forth against them; and this is what it says here, ״בקרובי אקדש״, and there it says ״הנגשים אל ה׳״, and these two expressions we find set adjacent in a single verse (Jeremiah 30:21): ״והקרבתיו ונגש אלי״. And the second, in the Tent of Meeting, where He said (Exodus 29:43): ״ונועדתי שמה לבני ישראל ונקדש בכבודי״ — and this is an allusion to the revelation of the Shekhinah to the sons of Aaron, in His saying ונקדש בכבודי: He mentioned ונקדש to allude to ״בקרובי אקדש״, and He mentioned בכבודי to allude to ״ועל פני כל העם אכבד״. And the word בקרובי is in the plural — that the Holy One, blessed be He, is sanctified through those near to Him, that is, through the things near to Him. But in a Torah scroll that is not vocalized it is possible to read בקרבי in the singular, and it would mean the angel near to Him: 'in one near to Me I am sanctified,' just like ״ונקדש בכבודי״; and you will find the word deficient of a vav — בקרבי is what is written — as in (Hosea 11:9): ״בקרבך קדוש״, and likewise (Deuteronomy 31:17): ״על כי אין אלהי בקרבי״. And this is because the attribute of justice struck them for not directing their intention toward the vav, as he mentioned above." "<b>And Aaron was silent.</b> Silence is one of the practices of mourning, and they are specified through Ezekiel, of blessed memory, as it is written (Ezekiel 24:16–17): ״בן אדם הנני לוקח ממך את מחמד עיניך במגפה ולא תספד ולא תבכה ולא תבוא דמעתך, האנק דם מתים אבל לא תעשה פארך חבוש עליך ונעליך תשים ברגליך ולא תעטה על שפם ולחם אנשים לא תאכל״, ״אמור לבית ישראל״ etc. ״הנני מחלל את מקדשי גאון עזכם מחמד עיניכם״ etc., ״ועשיתם כאשר עשיתי״ — for he was prophesying about the destruction of the Holy Temple, may it be rebuilt speedily in our days, and about the exile, that they would be exiled in the lands of their enemies and would not have sufficient means to complete and observe the laws of mourning. And it is written: ״והיה יחזקאל לכם למופת״." "<b>And know</b> that five things are included in 'but you shall not do': washing, anointing, marital relations, business transactions, and Torah study — for all of these are called joy, and joy is the opposite of mourning. And the joy of Torah and the joy of business you will find in a single verse, as it is written (Psalms 119:162): ״שש אנכי על אמרתך כמוצא שלל רב״ — this teaches that both are called joy; and it is written (Isaiah 9:2): ״כאשר יגילו בחלקם שלל״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:36.648388379Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}